VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-a11y
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: W4
ATTEMPT: 1
---
SCOPE: git diff master -- web/templates/components/dashboard-verdict-bar.html
(+18/-0, adds a "Living/Healthcare" decomposition line and a plain-English
verdict sentence beneath the existing headline, using
text-sm/text-gray-500/dark:text-gray-400/mt-1/space-y-0.5/num).

Method: read ACCESSIBILITY.md (16 numbered points, WCAG 2.2 AA baseline);
built the branch (`go build ./cmd/server`), ran it three times against
copies of testdata with monthly_living_expenses/monthly_healthcare tuned to
force each Health state (green/amber/red), and audited the live
`/dashboard` page with axe-core (via selenium-webdriver + axe-core source
injected directly, since @axe-core/cli defaults to headless Chrome's dark
`prefers-color-scheme`, which silently hid the light-theme failures on the
first pass — CDP `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` was used to force each theme
explicitly). Cross-checked every reported ratio against hand-computed WCAG
relative-luminance math. All servers were on 127.0.0.1 only and killed after
the audit; no personal financial data left running.

1. CONTRAST — checked in all three states the decomposition block can render
   in (HasTarget is only true for green/amber/red; it's never shown against
   the neutral gray-50 band), both themes.

   Backgrounds traced from internal/templates/render.go's verdictClasses map
   and internal/handlers/dashboard/verdict.go's Health selection
   (>10% of target over => red, else amber; under/on-budget => green):
     green:  bg-emerald-50  (#ecfdf5)      dark:bg-emerald-900/20 on body bg-gray-900
     amber:  bg-amber-50    (#fffbeb)      dark:bg-amber-900/20 on body bg-gray-900
     red:    bg-rose-50     (#fff1f2)      dark:bg-rose-900/20 on body bg-gray-900

   New text uses text-gray-500 (#6b7280) light / dark:text-gray-400 (#9ca3af)
   dark, text-sm (14px, normal weight — not "large text").

   Light theme, axe-confirmed via forced `prefers-color-scheme: light` on
   the live rendered page (dashboard-verdict-bar.html only):
     green (#ecfdf5 bg):  gray-500 => 4.59:1  PASS (≥4.5)
     amber (#fffbeb bg):  gray-500 => 4.66:1  PASS (≥4.5)  [hand-computed;
       axe run did not hit true amber because test data landed on green/red
       only — math uses the same verified formula that matched axe exactly
       in the other two states]
     red   (#fff1f2 bg):  gray-500 => 4.40:1  FAIL (<4.5)
       axe-core, live page, forced light scheme:
       "Element has insufficient color contrast of 4.4 (foreground color:
       #6b7280, background color: #fff1f2, font size: 10.5pt (14px), font
       weight: normal). Expected contrast ratio of 4.5:1" — hit on every
       text-gray-500-on-rose-50 node in the band, including the NEW
       decomposition `<div>Living: ... Healthcare: ...</div>` and the NEW
       `<div>No headroom — ... over plan for this period.</div>` sentence
       line added by this diff.
       Hand-computed (WCAG relative luminance): 4.400832:1. Matches axe
       exactly.

   Dark theme, axe-confirmed via forced `prefers-color-scheme: dark`:
     0 violations in the verdict band in either green or red state.
     Composited dark:text-gray-400 (#9ca3af) against the real composited
     background (dark:bg-*-900/20 over the body's dark:bg-gray-900,
     #111827):
       green: emerald-900/20 on gray-900 ≈ #0f232b -> 6.38:1  PASS
       red:   rose-900/20 on gray-900    ≈ #29172a -> 6.62:1  PASS
     Dark theme has full parity here (point 12) — no dark-only regression.

   RULING: point 7 (contrast ≥4.5:1) is violated in the red/light
   combination for the two new lines this diff adds.

   PROVENANCE — this exact defect (text-gray-500/dark:text-gray-400 on the
   rose-50 band background, 4.40:1) already exists in master, unrelated to
   this diff: line 16/19/22's pre-existing "over {{Months}} months" span
   uses the identical classes against the identical background, and the
   Spent/Target 10px uppercase labels (lines 48/52, unchanged) do too — axe
   flagged all of these together with the two new lines in the red state.
   The diff does not invent a new failing color pairing; it reuses the
   file's own already-broken utility-class combination and puts two more
   sentences behind it. Per the audit brief this is reported with its
   provenance, but per ACCESSIBILITY.md point 7 (a flat numeric threshold,
   no grandfather clause) the two new text nodes are live content that
   fails AA today, in a state (>10% over budget) that is the primary reason
   this feature exists — so it is called a FAIL rather than filed as a
   non-blocking note. The pre-existing occurrences (lines 16/19/22, 48, 52)
   are flagged as a pre-existing site-wide defect for the lead to fix
   alongside this task, not newly introduced by W4.

   Also observed (pre-existing, out of scope for this diff, not blocking
   this verdict): axe flagged text-emerald-600 (#059669) on bg-emerald-50
   (#ecfdf5) at 3.57:1 (Net Savings positive-value figure) and
   text-emerald-600/text-rose-600 value coloring against bg-rose-50 at
   3.43–4.27:1 — all pre-existing, unrelated to the decomposition/sentence
   feature, and unchanged by this diff.

2. COLOR-ALONE (point 8) — PASS. "Living: $X over" / "Healthcare: $Y
   under" / sentence Kinds "both"/"mixed"/"over" all spell the
   over/under/on-target distinction in words. The new spans do not apply
   any conditional color (no text-rose-*/text-emerald-* on the new content
   at all — everything in the new block is the same gray-500/gray-400
   regardless of over/under), so there isn't even redundant color-coding to
   verify parity on; the words are the only channel, which satisfies the
   point outright.

3. LINEARIZATION (points 1/3 spirit, no explicit ACCESSIBILITY.md number
   for this but it's WCAG 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence, AA-adjacent A) — PASS.
   grep confirms no `aria-hidden`, `aria-live`, `sr-only`, or
   `visually-hidden` anywhere in the file — no suppressed or
   screen-reader-only injected text. New block sits as a sibling `<div>`
   directly after the headline `<div>`, inside the same `flex-1` column,
   before the separate `ml-auto` stat-tiles column. DOM order (label →
   headline → decomposition → sentence → stat tiles) matches visual/reading
   order; no CSS `order` property is applied anywhere in this file.

4. `.num` CLASS (web/static/css/styles.css:149) — PASS.
   ```
   .num {
       font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, "SF Mono", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
       font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
       font-feature-settings: "tnum";
   }
   ```
   Only changes font-family and tabular-figure rendering; no font-size,
   line-height, width, or overflow rules. Size is inherited from the
   surrounding text-sm (Tailwind's rem-based 0.875rem), so 200% browser
   zoom / text-resize scales it with the rest of the paragraph — nothing in
   `.num` fights reflow (WCAG 1.4.4/1.4.10).

FAILURES:
- ACCESSIBILITY.md point 7 / WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.3 (Contrast, AA) —
  web/templates/components/dashboard-verdict-bar.html:26 and :39 (new
  decomposition `<div>` and new "No headroom" sentence `<div>`, both under
  the `text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-1 space-y-0.5` wrapper at
  line 26): light-theme contrast is 4.40:1 against the red-state
  `bg-rose-50` band background, below the 4.5:1 AA minimum for normal-size
  text. Confirmed by axe-core against the live rendered page and by hand
  computation (relative-luminance formula, both give 4.40:1/4.400832:1).
  Provenance: identical class/background pairing pre-exists in this same
  file (lines 16/19/22, 48, 52, unchanged by this diff) — the diff
  replicates a pre-existing defect into two new, frequently-visible lines
  rather than introducing a new failure category. Reported per the audit
  brief's provenance requirement; still a FAIL because the new content is
  live text that does not meet AA today, in the state (over budget by
  >10%) most central to the feature.

OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking, out of scope for this diff):
- Same page, pre-existing: text-emerald-600 (#059669) on bg-emerald-50 at
  3.57:1 (Net Savings figure, green state) and value coloring on bg-rose-50
  at 3.43–4.27:1 — unrelated to W4's decomposition/sentence feature,
